1
00:00:16,032 --> 00:00:22,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is the Samyutta Nikaya, chapter 22, verse 85.

2
00:00:22,778 --> 00:00:27,743
[SPEAKER_01]: This is called Yamaka, which is a bhikkhu Yamaka.

3
00:00:28,743 --> 00:00:40,274
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's in reference to his wrong view that he was holding, which was, he was going around saying, a bhikkhu whose defilements have ended, an arahant, at the breakup of the body,

4
00:00:41,043 --> 00:00:47,045
[SPEAKER_01]: He ceases, is destroyed and is not, is not or does not exist beyond or after death.

5
00:00:48,765 --> 00:00:51,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's many such wrong views.

6
00:00:51,826 --> 00:00:52,106
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

7
00:00:52,126 --> 00:00:57,228
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why is it wrong to state that he does not exist after death?

8
00:01:00,029 --> 00:01:04,570
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it states what's after death.

9
00:01:05,690 --> 00:01:07,951
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, after the break-up of the arguments.

10
00:01:09,582 --> 00:01:12,264
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's wrong simply because it's implying after.

11
00:01:16,448 --> 00:01:19,010
[SPEAKER_04]: No, that you know what's after, basically.

12
00:01:19,030 --> 00:01:20,531
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there is this after.

13
00:01:20,651 --> 00:01:21,352
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, no, he might not.

14
00:01:21,812 --> 00:01:23,053
[SPEAKER_04]: Maybe he knows, maybe he doesn't.

15
00:01:23,614 --> 00:01:24,355
[SPEAKER_04]: Obviously, he doesn't know.

16
00:01:24,375 --> 00:01:33,562
[SPEAKER_04]: But the thing is, it seems, according to the Sutta, that even the statement is wrong, regardless of whether it's... whether he knows or not.

17
00:01:34,303 --> 00:01:36,525
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why is it even wrong to state?

18
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:40,321
[SPEAKER_04]: that he does not exist after death.

19
00:01:41,421 --> 00:01:42,401
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you not state it?

20
00:01:42,761 --> 00:01:50,863
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you just... Is it okay to think for yourself as to what happens to Arahant after death?

21
00:01:52,223 --> 00:01:54,264
[SPEAKER_04]: Why are those thoughts a problem?

22
00:01:56,084 --> 00:01:59,085
[SPEAKER_04]: Why any views pertaining to such thoughts would be a problem?

23
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:12,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that which they're pertaining to, the arahant, he does not exist after death, as though he gets destroyed.

24
00:02:13,264 --> 00:02:26,608
[SPEAKER_04]: But even theoretically, say, if they're not necessarily implying the sense of self, if somebody's just trying to figure out what arahant is or would be, would these thoughts still be wrong?

25
00:02:28,368 --> 00:02:30,089
[SPEAKER_04]: And if so, then why?

26
00:02:33,299 --> 00:02:46,625
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody is just trying to... if somebody is trying to figure... basically, the only way to understand what an arahant is, is to... become one.

27
00:02:46,665 --> 00:02:48,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, to become one, basically.

28
00:02:48,226 --> 00:02:53,228
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, somebody who's not an arahant comes and asks an arahant, what happens after death?

29
00:02:54,168 --> 00:03:01,291
[SPEAKER_04]: Or rather, says, oh, so arahant then doesn't exist after death, or exists after death, or both doesn't exist and neither exists after death.

30
00:03:02,292 --> 00:03:02,492
[SPEAKER_04]: Why

31
00:03:03,012 --> 00:03:05,253
[SPEAKER_04]: Any of such statements would be wrong.

32
00:03:07,513 --> 00:03:10,894
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it because Arahant exists after death, or he doesn't, or neither, or both?

33
00:03:11,695 --> 00:03:15,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, wrong in a sense that it doesn't matter.

34
00:03:16,116 --> 00:03:17,997
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly.

35
00:03:18,057 --> 00:03:21,938
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it's wrong basically not because of what Arahant is or isn't.

36
00:03:22,098 --> 00:03:25,199
[SPEAKER_04]: It's wrong because the threshold of the question

37
00:03:26,377 --> 00:03:27,437
[SPEAKER_04]: implies existence.

38
00:03:28,638 --> 00:03:38,041
[SPEAKER_04]: So, whether there is arahant or not, or whatever else, for that person, on the base of that view and the question, they see nothing below existence of upādāna.

39
00:03:38,901 --> 00:03:40,182
[SPEAKER_04]: They see nothing below that.

40
00:03:40,602 --> 00:03:44,183
[SPEAKER_04]: So, question in and of itself is based on upādāna.

41
00:03:44,223 --> 00:03:47,464
[SPEAKER_04]: It's based on that which arahant isn't, doesn't have.

42
00:03:48,713 --> 00:03:56,060
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's why there is no right answer to it, because it's entirely top-to-bottom question based on a wrong assumption.

43
00:03:57,041 --> 00:04:06,491
[SPEAKER_04]: So, is, isn't, both is and isn't, neither is nor isn't, still focuses on the content of my answer and completely no self-awareness in regard to the question.

44
00:04:07,151 --> 00:04:12,517
[SPEAKER_04]: That that question presupposes already existence, bhava, which arahant is free from.

45
00:04:13,027 --> 00:04:14,428
[SPEAKER_00]: The basis of the question is wrong.

46
00:04:14,808 --> 00:04:15,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely, completely.

47
00:04:15,948 --> 00:04:17,169
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot have a right answer.

48
00:04:17,229 --> 00:04:20,131
[SPEAKER_04]: It cannot have a right answer, yeah, because it's a completely wrong question.

49
00:04:20,311 --> 00:04:33,297
[SPEAKER_04]: Any answer given to it, it would just basically then, maybe inadvertently, but it would still be affirming the wrong the wrong base of a question.

50
00:04:33,397 --> 00:04:38,300
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if somebody wants to then understand what ārahant

51
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:41,821
[SPEAKER_04]: What happens to an arahant after the body breaks apart?

52
00:04:42,521 --> 00:04:50,444
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, the only way to understand that is to remove every slight... the slightest traces of bhava in your mind, in your notions, from the five aggregates, basically.

53
00:04:51,345 --> 00:04:54,106
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, obviously, you wouldn't be asking that question, because you got your answer.

54
00:04:56,387 --> 00:05:01,949
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, you cannot approach it, you cannot understand it, you cannot see it, through an external standpoint.

55
00:05:02,469 --> 00:05:07,771
[SPEAKER_04]: And answer to such questions would be affirmation of the external standpoint to that situation.

56
00:05:08,968 --> 00:05:09,768
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's impossible.

57
00:05:10,329 --> 00:05:10,809
[SPEAKER_04]: Even...

58
00:05:10,829 --> 00:05:15,111
[SPEAKER_04]: So, see, like, even sotāpatthi is impossible through the external standpoint.

59
00:05:16,031 --> 00:05:22,275
[SPEAKER_04]: But through the various right external standpoints, you can narrow it down and then discern it internally.

60
00:05:22,715 --> 00:05:33,260
[SPEAKER_04]: But as you progress further to the path, in the path, and coming, like, to the point of arhantship, then there is simply no room for any external standpoint, even the right one.

61
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:37,302
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why arhant is himself beyond the right view as well.

62
00:05:39,703 --> 00:05:49,065
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, exactly like in the other suttas, if a question were to pertain to also what happens then in nibbāna, or after nibbāna, or part in nibbāna, it makes no sense.

63
00:05:49,365 --> 00:06:01,667
[SPEAKER_04]: The question makes no sense, because the lowest that question can go, it's still too much of bhava for any right answer to apply, so to speak.

64
00:06:01,687 --> 00:06:05,908
[SPEAKER_04]: So you need to undo the existence of it, the assumption of existence in it.

65
00:06:06,964 --> 00:06:12,166
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, as I said, then you would get the answer by undoing the question, not by providing an answer to the question.

66
00:06:14,967 --> 00:06:18,808
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just to mention some other views, similar.

67
00:06:20,469 --> 00:06:25,531
[SPEAKER_01]: The self is neither suffering or happy and is unafflicted after death.

68
00:06:26,371 --> 00:06:26,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Unafflicted?

69
00:06:27,031 --> 00:06:27,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Unafflicted.

70
00:06:27,932 --> 00:06:32,633
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, it's independent of feelings and everything that comes and goes.

71
00:06:32,653 --> 00:06:33,714
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why is that wrong?

72
00:06:37,312 --> 00:06:39,274
[SPEAKER_02]: It assumes an extratemporal self.

73
00:06:39,934 --> 00:06:40,455
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah.

74
00:06:40,555 --> 00:06:44,658
[SPEAKER_04]: But practically speaking, like say somebody's like, well, I don't know what extratemporal means.

75
00:06:45,419 --> 00:06:46,960
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just that's how it feels for me.

76
00:06:47,761 --> 00:06:51,804
[SPEAKER_04]: Myself is not affected by pain or pleasure and thus it will remain unaffected.

77
00:06:52,725 --> 00:07:01,633
[SPEAKER_04]: Is there a way that somebody who holds their wrong view can verify for themselves without just accepting your view now of extratemporal self-explanation?

78
00:07:02,447 --> 00:07:08,530
[SPEAKER_04]: Can I see a contradiction, a discrepancy, in the very view I'm holding, that can then show me that you are right?

79
00:07:09,931 --> 00:07:12,972
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you in control of the things that make up your sense of self?

80
00:07:13,472 --> 00:07:18,915
[SPEAKER_04]: So, feelings... Yeah, so it's basically, yeah, one base would be the lack of control.

81
00:07:19,375 --> 00:07:27,278
[SPEAKER_04]: Another thing would be, okay, you might have notions about yourself not being affected, but can you even conceive yourself, your sense of self, apart from any feeling?

82
00:07:27,539 --> 00:07:28,539
[SPEAKER_04]: Have you ever conceived it?

83
00:07:29,082 --> 00:07:30,343
[SPEAKER_04]: That you have not felt something.

84
00:07:31,083 --> 00:07:31,523
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know.

85
00:07:31,723 --> 00:07:33,504
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's it's an absolute fantasy.

86
00:07:34,105 --> 00:07:37,426
[SPEAKER_04]: Even right here, right now, as I'm thinking it, I'm feeling something.

87
00:07:38,467 --> 00:07:44,310
[SPEAKER_04]: So, can I even imagine, exactly, a thought of sense of self without a feeling?

88
00:07:44,410 --> 00:07:45,931
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's inconceivable.

89
00:07:46,371 --> 00:07:49,693
[SPEAKER_04]: So, my view is truly inconceivable here, while I'm stating it.

90
00:07:50,773 --> 00:07:58,057
[SPEAKER_04]: And and on the basis of that, if somebody were honest, they could see that as a obviously insurmountable contradiction and they would have to then give up that view.

91
00:07:59,817 --> 00:08:06,439
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's kind of the only way you can really... Of course, if somebody is more authentic than the others, you know, they will need less instruction, less convincing.

92
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:20,184
[SPEAKER_04]: But fundamentally, the only way to really approve the wrong view, or wrong views of all sorts of kinds, is to show to the person that holds it, if they're authentic enough, to show them that, in and of itself, it's a contradiction in terms.

93
00:08:21,245 --> 00:08:24,206
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, not simply by offering another, better view.

94
00:08:24,366 --> 00:08:28,027
[SPEAKER_04]: Because then, they might even take your view then, but they're still taking it wrongly.

95
00:08:28,812 --> 00:08:32,375
[SPEAKER_04]: They still don't... well, they don't know if it's... if it's right for themselves.

96
00:08:32,575 --> 00:08:34,297
[SPEAKER_04]: They just took it because it sounds better now.

97
00:08:35,658 --> 00:08:48,829
[SPEAKER_04]: But you really want them to know that it's right, in and of itself, because it's free from contradiction, free from anything that you would not be able to verify here and now for yourself as, okay, this... there's no discrepancy, this adds up, this doesn't add up, and so on.

98
00:08:50,030 --> 00:08:52,172
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in this particular case, it's impossible.

99
00:08:53,653 --> 00:08:54,674
[SPEAKER_04]: It was impossible.

100
00:08:54,994 --> 00:08:56,135
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just inconceivable.

101
00:08:56,932 --> 00:09:05,979
[SPEAKER_04]: Despite how it feels, how your sense of self feels, it's inconceivable to even imagine, because that imagination is underlined by something you're currently feeling.

102
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:20,230
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you saying that sense of self is independent of good or bad feeling, whatever comes this way, simply does not actually match even your own experience of that sense of self, which is always, always paired with feeling, with perception.

103
00:09:22,665 --> 00:09:32,190
[SPEAKER_04]: And that pleasing thought, that fantasy, that image of that sense of self, independent of all these things, there's feeling underneath that as well, while you're saying it.

104
00:09:33,190 --> 00:09:34,251
[SPEAKER_04]: And if you're honest, you'll see it.

105
00:09:34,991 --> 00:09:41,054
[SPEAKER_04]: And you realize, well, then, it cannot be independent, regardless of how much it appears to be so.

106
00:09:41,094 --> 00:09:43,936
[SPEAKER_04]: It only appears to be so, because you've been cultivating such assumptions.

107
00:09:46,297 --> 00:09:47,938
[SPEAKER_02]: Or how much you want it to be so.

108
00:09:48,338 --> 00:09:50,479
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, how much you want it, yeah.

109
00:09:53,098 --> 00:09:57,241
[SPEAKER_01]: And in another sutta, it gives a few other examples.

110
00:09:57,461 --> 00:09:58,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Self exists for me.

111
00:10:00,083 --> 00:10:01,363
[SPEAKER_01]: This is true and certain.

112
00:10:01,644 --> 00:10:02,664
[SPEAKER_02]: That's for sure.

113
00:10:03,825 --> 00:10:04,866
[SPEAKER_01]: No self exists for me.

114
00:10:05,266 --> 00:10:06,427
[SPEAKER_01]: I perceive self with self.

115
00:10:06,687 --> 00:10:07,968
[SPEAKER_01]: I perceive not-self with self.

116
00:10:08,088 --> 00:10:09,289
[SPEAKER_01]: I perceive self with not-self.

117
00:10:10,069 --> 00:10:14,092
[SPEAKER_01]: It is this self of mine that speaks and feels and experiences.

118
00:10:15,142 --> 00:10:16,463
[SPEAKER_01]: The result of good and bad actions.

119
00:10:16,823 --> 00:10:24,027
[SPEAKER_01]: But the self of mine is permanent, everlasting, eternal, not subject to change, and it will endure as long as eternity.

120
00:10:24,328 --> 00:10:24,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

121
00:10:24,728 --> 00:10:25,368
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's the problem?

122
00:10:25,809 --> 00:10:26,709
[SPEAKER_04]: What's the problem with that?

123
00:10:26,749 --> 00:10:31,112
[SPEAKER_04]: So, self exists for me, self doesn't exist for me, self both, self neither.

124
00:10:31,272 --> 00:10:32,573
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the problem with that statement?

125
00:10:38,096 --> 00:10:40,578
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, there are a few problems, but let's come up with some.

126
00:10:42,579 --> 00:10:55,603
[SPEAKER_00]: The basic one is that, yeah, again, the basis of any of those statements is already a statement about assuming self, basically, or taking taking self as a thing.

127
00:10:55,623 --> 00:10:58,424
[SPEAKER_00]: So, saying self exists for me.

128
00:10:59,904 --> 00:11:00,964
[SPEAKER_04]: Self doesn't exist.

129
00:11:00,984 --> 00:11:03,145
[SPEAKER_04]: Self-bought in other means that exists for you.

130
00:11:03,245 --> 00:11:03,625
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

131
00:11:04,156 --> 00:11:07,619
[SPEAKER_04]: And the main problem with this view is that that's where the self actually is.

132
00:11:07,999 --> 00:11:11,482
[SPEAKER_04]: It's on the level of existence, not on the level where you say self is.

133
00:11:11,942 --> 00:11:13,484
[SPEAKER_04]: So, self exists for me.

134
00:11:13,624 --> 00:11:14,685
[SPEAKER_04]: No self exists for me.

135
00:11:14,785 --> 00:11:15,646
[SPEAKER_04]: Either way, no.

136
00:11:15,826 --> 00:11:19,008
[SPEAKER_04]: Self exists for you because of your statement of existence.

137
00:11:19,749 --> 00:11:25,353
[SPEAKER_04]: Whether that statement of existence is then referred to the sense of self, or both, or neither, that's irrelevant.

138
00:11:25,854 --> 00:11:27,856
[SPEAKER_04]: That's already the secondary result.

139
00:11:29,529 --> 00:11:33,371
[SPEAKER_04]: Self very much exists, because things exist in that bhava sense.

140
00:11:34,111 --> 00:11:39,154
[SPEAKER_04]: Existence is the self that already exists for you, when you say that that self over there exists for me.

141
00:11:40,154 --> 00:11:44,736
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's simply not where the sense of self is, and you're overlooking it, because it's on the basis of existence.

142
00:11:46,237 --> 00:11:57,143
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why it comes down to then, exactly like, oh, with self, he perceives self, I, with existence, he perceives what he thinks self is, which is then double self.

143
00:11:58,120 --> 00:12:03,744
[SPEAKER_04]: With self, he perceives not-self, but again, still, his experience is rooted in not-self.

144
00:12:04,224 --> 00:12:16,612
[SPEAKER_04]: Or then, if he put the emphasis on that, what he thinks self is, then with non-existence, with like a not-self, in a way, he can be perceiving either way, there will have to be a reference point of self.

145
00:12:16,632 --> 00:12:17,713
[SPEAKER_04]: Either here or there or both.

146
00:12:18,553 --> 00:12:19,774
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's in both.

147
00:12:19,814 --> 00:12:22,876
[SPEAKER_04]: It's in existing of the self is already the self.

148
00:12:23,972 --> 00:12:26,714
[SPEAKER_04]: Plus the self that you now assumed even further to exist.

149
00:12:27,354 --> 00:12:27,874
[SPEAKER_04]: So, whether you...

150
00:12:27,915 --> 00:12:34,359
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, that's what I mean, like, whether you deny or affirm the sense of self, you still very much hold the sense of self, because you very much hold the existence.

151
00:12:34,859 --> 00:12:39,822
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if you don't define it as a sense of self, it will be yourself.

152
00:12:39,842 --> 00:12:44,225
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why exactly when they did sometimes ask the Buddha, also Master Gautama says there is no self.

153
00:12:45,932 --> 00:12:47,393
[SPEAKER_04]: There is no self.

154
00:12:48,074 --> 00:12:49,314
[SPEAKER_04]: The no-self exists.

155
00:12:49,574 --> 00:13:01,081
[SPEAKER_04]: So, either way, the root, the basis of the self-view, the existence, the bhava, would remain completely unchanged, regardless of what answer the Master Gautama would give them.

156
00:13:01,121 --> 00:13:02,662
[SPEAKER_04]: So, he would not say anything.

157
00:13:02,682 --> 00:13:03,583
[SPEAKER_04]: He would just remain quiet.

158
00:13:04,840 --> 00:13:08,602
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's a completely wrong assumption, wrong question.

159
00:13:09,083 --> 00:13:14,807
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like adding another contradiction on top of what is already a contradiction, basically.

160
00:13:14,827 --> 00:13:14,927
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

161
00:13:14,967 --> 00:13:18,009
[SPEAKER_04]: Which then they would take as the right answer.

162
00:13:18,269 --> 00:13:21,411
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's simply... it's a loaded question, basically.

163
00:13:21,431 --> 00:13:22,812
[SPEAKER_04]: A question loaded with existence.

164
00:13:22,992 --> 00:13:26,574
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you cannot answer it correctly without dismantling the existence.

165
00:13:27,115 --> 00:13:28,576
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's not what the question pertains to.

166
00:13:29,556 --> 00:13:30,577
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, yeah.

167
00:13:32,707 --> 00:13:36,788
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a lot of these views are followed by this kind of statement.

168
00:13:37,608 --> 00:13:49,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Having such a view, one is in a jungle of views, which is difficult to pass through, and leaves one wriggling, swaying in this way and that, struggling and fettered.

169
00:13:49,631 --> 00:13:52,791
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just going from... there's no end to those speculations and so on.

170
00:13:52,811 --> 00:13:54,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you just keep getting entangled.

171
00:13:54,632 --> 00:13:55,712
[SPEAKER_04]: Whichever way you twist it.

172
00:13:57,192 --> 00:14:04,034
[SPEAKER_01]: So, back to Bhikkhu Yamaka and his view about the arahant not existing after death.

173
00:14:05,855 --> 00:14:11,297
[SPEAKER_01]: So, other bhikkhus tried to persuade him otherwise, but he didn't want to abandon that view.

174
00:14:13,018 --> 00:14:17,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, Venerable Sariputta came and asked him some questions.

175
00:14:20,380 --> 00:14:25,082
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the evening, the Venerable Sariputta approached the Venerable Yamaka and asked him, what do you think

176
00:14:26,925 --> 00:14:30,488
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he's not answering, he's not saying if the arahant exists or not.

177
00:14:31,348 --> 00:14:32,649
[SPEAKER_01]: He's just asking him questions.

178
00:14:33,710 --> 00:14:34,351
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think?

179
00:14:34,951 --> 00:14:36,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Is form permanent or impermanent?

180
00:14:37,850 --> 00:14:38,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Impermanent, sir.

181
00:14:38,931 --> 00:14:41,552
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's impermanent, is it suffering or happiness?

182
00:14:41,972 --> 00:14:42,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering.

183
00:14:43,253 --> 00:14:48,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Since it's impermanent, suffering and subject to change, is it fit to be regarded thus?

184
00:14:48,216 --> 00:14:48,776
[SPEAKER_01]: This is mine.

185
00:14:49,156 --> 00:14:49,536
[SPEAKER_01]: I am this.

186
00:14:49,657 --> 00:14:50,317
[SPEAKER_01]: This is myself.

187
00:14:50,857 --> 00:14:51,377
[SPEAKER_01]: This is no.

188
00:14:52,218 --> 00:14:54,259
[SPEAKER_01]: So, likewise with the other aggregates.

189
00:14:54,899 --> 00:14:57,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling, perception, tensions, consciousness.

190
00:14:58,441 --> 00:14:59,162
[SPEAKER_04]: So, okay.

191
00:14:59,362 --> 00:15:00,242
[SPEAKER_04]: Let's stop there now.

192
00:15:03,555 --> 00:15:08,999
[SPEAKER_04]: So, Venerable Sariputta is then not going to answer the questions that Bhikkhu Yāmaka asked.

193
00:15:09,940 --> 00:15:15,204
[SPEAKER_04]: He's gonna go and question him about the validity of his questions, which is what we've been saying.

194
00:15:16,345 --> 00:15:19,847
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why would then he bring the five aggregates?

195
00:15:20,147 --> 00:15:21,228
[SPEAKER_04]: Why would he talk about that?

196
00:15:23,070 --> 00:15:29,935
[SPEAKER_04]: In light of what we just said, why would that be the right way to go about these questions?

197
00:15:33,230 --> 00:15:40,217
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, as I said before, the... so, he's not giving an explanation.

198
00:15:40,757 --> 00:15:52,228
[SPEAKER_02]: He's... he's inviting him to discern in his presently enjoying experience the nature of this sense of self that he's referring or that he's implying in his question.

199
00:15:52,849 --> 00:15:53,610
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

200
00:15:54,030 --> 00:15:57,694
[SPEAKER_04]: But, yeah, so... but why would then... why would he talk about form and feeling?

201
00:15:59,323 --> 00:16:18,671
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in your presently enjoying experience, do you have control over the fact that you will die, for example, or you don't have any control over...

202
00:16:18,691 --> 00:16:20,491
[SPEAKER_04]: Right, but he wasn't talking about death, though.

203
00:16:20,711 --> 00:16:24,353
[SPEAKER_04]: He was talking about what's there living, the five aggregates.

204
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:26,681
[SPEAKER_04]: And why, why is that suitable?

205
00:16:26,701 --> 00:16:30,265
[SPEAKER_02]: Or did you have any say in, in this form?

206
00:16:30,325 --> 00:16:39,495
[SPEAKER_04]: So, some must, the vikāyāmaka, or somebody who has that view, could say, well, yeah, I don't, I don't control form, feeling, none of that, but my sense of self is independent of these things.

207
00:16:40,856 --> 00:16:41,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

208
00:16:44,941 --> 00:16:47,844
[SPEAKER_04]: But, ehm... How do then, how do you then dislodge?

209
00:16:50,732 --> 00:16:53,494
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that independence implies permanency.

210
00:16:54,254 --> 00:16:57,096
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there's two things involved.

211
00:16:57,336 --> 00:16:59,057
[SPEAKER_03]: The identification with the aggregates.

212
00:17:00,022 --> 00:17:07,524
[SPEAKER_03]: And regarding that as self, which is possible only because those aggregates are regarded as permanent.

213
00:17:07,744 --> 00:17:12,345
[SPEAKER_03]: So, to then question the aggregates and explicitly, do you see that as impermanent?

214
00:17:12,365 --> 00:17:14,065
[SPEAKER_03]: Then he says, yes, they are impermanent.

215
00:17:14,845 --> 00:17:23,327
[SPEAKER_03]: There being... the only thing there is, being those five aggregates, if all that's impermanent, then that independency cannot stand.

216
00:17:23,347 --> 00:17:24,207
[SPEAKER_04]: Cannot stand, exactly.

217
00:17:24,647 --> 00:17:27,788
[SPEAKER_04]: But what we said in regard to that view of self and the feeling,

218
00:17:28,788 --> 00:17:49,432
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, you start questioning aggregates, because, again, you would point out to that person that, well, as independent as you might feel, as concerned about what's after you might be, can you experience that concern, that independence of self or whatever, without feeling, without this feeling underneath it, without this form, without this intention?

219
00:17:49,593 --> 00:17:50,193
[SPEAKER_04]: Inconceivable.

220
00:17:50,213 --> 00:17:51,733
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, even in your imagination,

221
00:17:52,221 --> 00:17:59,049
[SPEAKER_04]: The utmost, eternal, ātman, projected, sense of self, ever enduring, again, as the Buddha said, cannot be apart from the six sense base.

222
00:17:59,590 --> 00:18:04,215
[SPEAKER_04]: If somebody claims, I'll show you the all apart from this, that's simply not factually possible.

223
00:18:04,996 --> 00:18:06,818
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it would have to be on the basis of this.

224
00:18:07,038 --> 00:18:08,159
[SPEAKER_04]: And what is the basis of this?

225
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:12,905
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, it's the form, it's the feeling, it's the perception, intention, and then

226
00:18:13,881 --> 00:18:25,913
[SPEAKER_04]: If now a person is clear that, regardless of what view they have, that view is simultaneously always paired with this, with the five aggregates, or one of them, whichever way you would have emphasized it.

227
00:18:26,433 --> 00:18:32,719
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, by questioning the basis of the five aggregates, you realize that, oh well, it's inconceivable for this to be apart from it.

228
00:18:32,919 --> 00:18:35,442
[SPEAKER_04]: It's inconceivable for this to exist independently.

229
00:18:36,328 --> 00:18:45,538
[SPEAKER_04]: Any notion of existence, independent existence of the five aggregates, is exactly... it's based on this notion of ignoring the fact that it cannot be apart from the five aggregates.

230
00:18:46,159 --> 00:18:53,007
[SPEAKER_04]: And it cannot be apart from that which is thoroughly, top to bottom, impermanent, subject to change, unownable,

231
00:18:53,487 --> 00:18:58,409
[SPEAKER_04]: Unpleasant, then, it cannot be taken as mine, as an independent self that can exist apart from this.

232
00:18:58,629 --> 00:19:00,030
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know exactly what I was asking now.

233
00:19:00,050 --> 00:19:06,332
[SPEAKER_04]: It's because I'm sure many people would have read the sutta and read the suttas like, oh, you see these views, but then it seems almost like a leap.

234
00:19:06,372 --> 00:19:07,313
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, now you just aggregate.

235
00:19:07,333 --> 00:19:07,753
[SPEAKER_04]: It's okay.

236
00:19:08,333 --> 00:19:11,054
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's like with the connection between the two.

237
00:19:11,275 --> 00:19:20,318
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, because if you were to really understand this, the way the Sariputta was asking him, you would then be free from any notion of an external sense of self or conceit.

238
00:19:22,860 --> 00:19:29,982
[SPEAKER_04]: So, but if you think you're understanding the questioning on aggregates, while still have your sense of self intact, then you are missing something.

239
00:19:30,062 --> 00:19:30,883
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's what I'm pointing out.

240
00:19:30,903 --> 00:19:37,425
[SPEAKER_04]: You're missing that connection, that sort of clear reasoning that has been pushed

241
00:19:38,557 --> 00:19:59,289
[SPEAKER_04]: far enough whereby you cannot now ignore the fact that doesn't matter how lofty, how remote, how independent, how eternal, how permanent your sense of self might feel, in regard to this or that, that, in and of itself, it's still another experience as a whole, that it's experience of the five aggregates.

242
00:20:00,090 --> 00:20:04,192
[SPEAKER_04]: So, by re-including it in that which it cannot be outside of,

243
00:20:06,285 --> 00:20:09,428
[SPEAKER_04]: Such reflections about impermanence of aggregates begin to apply.

244
00:20:11,229 --> 00:20:16,033
[SPEAKER_04]: Begin to actually undermine your sense of self, of that existence, of that question that implies existence.

245
00:20:18,195 --> 00:20:34,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and he goes on to say to Yamaka, so you should see any kind of form, or the five aggregates, any kind of five aggregates, past, future, present, internal, external, coarsifying, inferior or superior, far or near,

246
00:20:35,378 --> 00:20:40,401
[SPEAKER_01]: All the aggregates, with right understanding, this is not mine, I am not this, this is not myself.

247
00:20:42,242 --> 00:20:47,524
[SPEAKER_01]: And seeing this, a noble disciple grows disenchanted with the aggregates.

248
00:20:48,525 --> 00:20:50,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Being disenchanted, desire fades away.

249
00:20:51,306 --> 00:20:54,228
[SPEAKER_01]: When desire fades away, they are freed, liberated.

250
00:20:54,768 --> 00:20:57,169
[SPEAKER_01]: When they are freed, they know they are freed.

251
00:20:58,370 --> 00:21:01,492
[SPEAKER_01]: They then understand birth is ended, their life has been completed.

252
00:21:01,712 --> 00:21:01,992
[SPEAKER_01]: What has

253
00:21:02,827 --> 00:21:04,047
[SPEAKER_01]: To be done has been done.

254
00:21:04,067 --> 00:21:06,208
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no returning to any state of existence.

255
00:21:11,790 --> 00:21:14,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and he continues to question Yamaka.

256
00:21:14,832 --> 00:21:20,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you regard form as the Tathāgata, form as the Arahant?

257
00:21:21,414 --> 00:21:22,174
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, no.

258
00:21:22,535 --> 00:21:25,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you regard feeling, perceptions, intentions, consciousness as the Arahant?

259
00:21:25,976 --> 00:21:26,116
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

260
00:21:28,224 --> 00:21:28,904
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think?

261
00:21:29,525 --> 00:21:33,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you regard the arahant as in-form or outside-form?

262
00:21:33,747 --> 00:21:36,169
[SPEAKER_01]: In-feeling or outside-feeling?

263
00:21:36,509 --> 00:21:41,072
[SPEAKER_01]: In-perception, outside-perception, in-attention, outside-attention, in-consciousness, outside-consciousness.

264
00:21:43,213 --> 00:21:50,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you regard form, feeling, perceptions, intentions?

265
00:21:50,798 --> 00:21:52,479
[SPEAKER_04]: What's the problem with in?

266
00:21:53,900 --> 00:21:57,222
[SPEAKER_04]: You can say like, okay, I don't regard it outside of the aggregates, but it's in them.

267
00:21:58,298 --> 00:21:59,259
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is that wrong as well?

268
00:22:00,940 --> 00:22:04,542
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it implies an outside.

269
00:22:04,702 --> 00:22:05,963
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

270
00:22:06,724 --> 00:22:14,048
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you placed it inside and the only way you can place something inside is by assuming it being separate from it first.

271
00:22:14,068 --> 00:22:15,109
[SPEAKER_03]: I, outside of it.

272
00:22:15,970 --> 00:22:16,230
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

273
00:22:22,616 --> 00:22:24,158
[SPEAKER_01]: He continues the question.

274
00:22:24,318 --> 00:22:33,305
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think, do you regard form, feeling, perceptions, intentions, consciousness, taken together as the arahant, as the Self?

275
00:22:34,326 --> 00:22:34,827
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole thing.

276
00:22:35,047 --> 00:22:35,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

277
00:22:35,287 --> 00:22:36,528
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the Self.

278
00:22:38,410 --> 00:22:39,651
[SPEAKER_01]: And he answers... No.

279
00:22:39,691 --> 00:22:42,573
[SPEAKER_03]: If they individually cannot be as a group, they cannot be either.

280
00:22:43,254 --> 00:22:48,618
[SPEAKER_04]: Comprised of five heaps that remain the same, unchanged, regardless of whether together or alone.

281
00:22:49,980 --> 00:22:50,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Says...

282
00:22:51,882 --> 00:22:59,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think the arahants is one who is... or the Self is one with... who is without the aggregates?

283
00:22:59,808 --> 00:23:02,249
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, again, implies the independent separation, yeah.

284
00:23:03,010 --> 00:23:03,851
[SPEAKER_04]: And he says no, yeah.

285
00:23:05,212 --> 00:23:12,577
[SPEAKER_01]: But friend, we're not finding that the view of the Tathāgata thing, or the Self, is true or definite.

286
00:23:13,517 --> 00:23:14,098
[SPEAKER_01]: It... or

287
00:23:15,394 --> 00:23:20,455
[SPEAKER_01]: We're not finding that the view of self is true, definite, independent.

288
00:23:21,416 --> 00:23:23,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it fitting for you to declare, as I am?

289
00:23:23,676 --> 00:23:26,357
[SPEAKER_04]: It's independence and truthfulness.

290
00:23:26,377 --> 00:23:33,079
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's exactly what we said, just in regard to that self and feeling.

291
00:23:34,079 --> 00:23:41,121
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, if you're honest, if you stop doing it as well, regardless of how lofty you might feel, exactly, there is still feeling present.

292
00:23:41,141 --> 00:23:42,321
[SPEAKER_04]: You cannot even conceive

293
00:23:43,014 --> 00:23:51,037
[SPEAKER_04]: Any notion, the slightest notion of I am or the self, without without feeling being there, impossible.

294
00:23:51,077 --> 00:24:03,042
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I must not ignore that context, that it's... whenever I think about the sense of self, and the great self, and the big self, and the higher self, and myself, and inferior self, past self, future self, far self, all I'm thinking,

295
00:24:03,942 --> 00:24:04,462
[SPEAKER_04]: is exactly.

296
00:24:04,682 --> 00:24:12,286
[SPEAKER_04]: Thoughts, there are five aggregates that have, sure, certain valued content of the independence, which is a contradiction in terms.

297
00:24:12,886 --> 00:24:20,229
[SPEAKER_04]: Fundamentally, all I'm thinking is an image, is a feeling, is an intention, it's the form, it's a thought, it's basically the five aggregates.

298
00:24:20,269 --> 00:24:21,330
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's how

299
00:24:21,865 --> 00:24:29,389
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't just stop thinking the sense of self, but you can actually expand the context in regard to the thought of the sense of self.

300
00:24:30,309 --> 00:24:39,934
[SPEAKER_04]: And one would be expanding that context rightly, if one does not double down and blindly commit to the view that pertains to that already wrongly assumed external sense of self.

301
00:24:41,403 --> 00:24:51,268
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that person will not be establishing the context, because they have set themselves in external view that my sense of self is independent of all of this and its eternal everlasting.

302
00:24:51,849 --> 00:24:58,553
[SPEAKER_04]: And even if you don't think in such coarse terms, do you think simply that everything will work out forever after?

303
00:24:59,733 --> 00:25:02,555
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you have an implication that whatever you're doing, it's just heading up?

304
00:25:03,428 --> 00:25:11,832
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's already an external point of view that you assumed in the sense of everything will be fine regardless of whether it is pleasure or pain experienced here and now.

305
00:25:12,832 --> 00:25:27,438
[SPEAKER_04]: But that thought of after, of the external, of, you know, independence and safety... How can that be safe when the only way you can know it is on account of these five aggregates that are very unsafe, so to speak?

306
00:25:29,139 --> 00:25:31,800
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you were honest, you would realize that your idea of the highest

307
00:25:32,726 --> 00:25:42,428
[SPEAKER_04]: Heaven and deity, well, might be good, but in and of itself, it's still within this, which is not in my control, which is subject to change.

308
00:25:42,448 --> 00:25:46,029
[SPEAKER_04]: Thus, this too will have to change then.

309
00:25:46,149 --> 00:25:47,669
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it cannot be the eternal safety.

310
00:25:48,270 --> 00:25:53,151
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the notion of eternity exists only on account of these non-eternal five aggregates.

311
00:25:55,291 --> 00:25:58,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the, like, the difficulty is to see

312
00:26:00,198 --> 00:26:09,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Both, at the same time, and to see the nature of it also, as in, like, you can have a... you can... it's very obvious, let's say, I can think about...

313
00:26:10,002 --> 00:26:12,123
[SPEAKER_00]: I can think about feeling being impermanent.

314
00:26:12,543 --> 00:26:14,464
[SPEAKER_00]: I can say, oh, well, that can't be myself.

315
00:26:14,524 --> 00:26:15,965
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't be feeling.

316
00:26:16,045 --> 00:26:23,668
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, at the same time, to see that that self is dependent or within, cannot be without feeling, is...

317
00:26:24,523 --> 00:26:30,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can sort of see that one, but at the same time to see that the feeling being impermanent totally undermines the feeling.

318
00:26:30,887 --> 00:26:40,452
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why we always go through these five, because you might not be taking the feeling at the time when you're looking at the feeling, means you're taking some other aggregate, or all other aggregates.

319
00:26:40,532 --> 00:26:45,875
[SPEAKER_04]: You know, just see that this thought, that feeling is impermanent, is still undermined by the feeling.

320
00:26:45,975 --> 00:26:50,078
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah, even on that level, but you say sometimes just whatever, for whatever reason,

321
00:26:50,698 --> 00:26:54,659
[SPEAKER_04]: Your sense of self is just wiggling out of the feeling and it just doesn't feel like feeling is yours.

322
00:26:55,280 --> 00:26:57,160
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, can you know that sense of self?

323
00:26:57,200 --> 00:26:57,901
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not perceived.

324
00:26:58,221 --> 00:26:58,361
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

325
00:26:58,561 --> 00:26:59,301
[SPEAKER_04]: Ah, so then what?

326
00:26:59,361 --> 00:27:00,181
[SPEAKER_04]: Perception is yours?

327
00:27:00,621 --> 00:27:01,042
[SPEAKER_03]: No, it's not.

328
00:27:01,162 --> 00:27:08,704
[SPEAKER_04]: Ah, so it doesn't matter then what shape and form my assumption of sense of self can assume, it's going to be one or all of the five of the aggregates or one among them.

329
00:27:08,864 --> 00:27:09,945
[SPEAKER_04]: It cannot be outside of them.

330
00:27:10,817 --> 00:27:11,658
[SPEAKER_04]: Just inconceivable.

331
00:27:12,158 --> 00:27:24,127
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, if it were truly independent of the five aggregates, it would be independent of designation, independent of an image, independent of being known, independent of being conscious of.

332
00:27:24,308 --> 00:27:26,609
[SPEAKER_04]: It would not exist, basically.

333
00:27:26,629 --> 00:27:29,572
[SPEAKER_04]: You would not be able to talk about it.

334
00:27:37,538 --> 00:27:40,040
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, Abhigaviyāmaka, he finally,

335
00:27:41,952 --> 00:27:42,733
[SPEAKER_01]: He changes his mind.

336
00:27:44,354 --> 00:27:46,956
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, he's abandoned that view.

337
00:27:47,556 --> 00:27:59,186
[SPEAKER_01]: And then Sariputta asks him, so, if others were to ask you, when a bhikkhu is an arahant, whose taints are destroyed, what happens to him with the breakup of the body after death?

338
00:27:59,926 --> 00:28:00,587
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you answer?

339
00:28:02,208 --> 00:28:08,193
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, if they were to ask me that, I would answer, friends, the form is impermanent.

340
00:28:08,867 --> 00:28:10,388
[SPEAKER_01]: What is impermanent is suffering.

341
00:28:10,508 --> 00:28:13,750
[SPEAKER_01]: What is suffering has ceased and passed away.

342
00:28:13,770 --> 00:28:15,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with feeling, perceptions, intentions, consciousness.

343
00:28:18,753 --> 00:28:19,273
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

344
00:28:20,674 --> 00:28:20,854
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

345
00:28:21,134 --> 00:28:21,754
[SPEAKER_04]: He would not...

346
00:28:22,235 --> 00:28:33,081
[SPEAKER_04]: If somebody were to ask him, he would not give them an answer that pertains to that question, but he would actually show that the question inevitably is rooted in a contradiction of ownership and permanence in regard to the five aggregates.

347
00:28:35,634 --> 00:28:37,855
[SPEAKER_03]: So, Yamaka became one of the noble ones just there.

348
00:28:38,275 --> 00:28:39,336
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

349
00:28:40,957 --> 00:28:42,577
[SPEAKER_03]: No longer assuming... Yeah, yeah.

350
00:28:42,858 --> 00:28:44,378
[SPEAKER_04]: No longer being able to assume that.

351
00:28:44,618 --> 00:28:44,819
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

352
00:28:45,459 --> 00:28:52,002
[SPEAKER_04]: The external independence in regard to sense of self, whether he projected it into the Buddha, in Arhant or others, doesn't matter.

353
00:28:52,262 --> 00:28:59,786
[SPEAKER_03]: Doesn't have the usual stock phrases that are sometimes connected with, like, Sotāpatti, but for him to come up with that kind of answer,

354
00:29:00,255 --> 00:29:05,476
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, well, I think maybe he doesn't have the usual phrases, because he might not have been completely like a suttavā either.

355
00:29:06,456 --> 00:29:09,257
[SPEAKER_04]: He might have kind of, you know, understood.

356
00:29:09,277 --> 00:29:14,498
[SPEAKER_04]: Because he obviously was able to understand the questioning in regard to the aggregates, which means he was fairly familiar with that.

357
00:29:15,158 --> 00:29:25,920
[SPEAKER_04]: He was just probably an oversight on his part, fell into a view, and then just tried to, for whatever reason, argue the point from the assumed view, ignoring what he kind of already knew, in a way, yeah.

358
00:29:28,373 --> 00:29:39,078
[SPEAKER_01]: And Venerable Sariputta says good, and then says he will make up a simile for him, in order to convey the same meaning even more clearly.

359
00:29:41,659 --> 00:29:47,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Says, suppose there was a householder, a rich man with much wealth and property, protected by a bodyguard.

360
00:29:48,803 --> 00:29:53,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Then some man would appear, who wanted to ruin him, to harm him, to take his life.

361
00:29:53,985 --> 00:29:55,086
[SPEAKER_01]: It would occur to that man,

362
00:29:55,385 --> 00:29:59,668
[SPEAKER_01]: This householder is a rich man, with much wealth and property, protected by a bodyguard.

363
00:30:00,709 --> 00:30:02,870
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't be easy to take his life by force.

364
00:30:03,011 --> 00:30:05,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me get close to him and then take his life.

365
00:30:06,453 --> 00:30:10,216
[SPEAKER_01]: He would then approach that householder and say, I would like to serve you, sir.

366
00:30:10,956 --> 00:30:13,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Then that householder would appoint him as a servant.

367
00:30:14,179 --> 00:30:22,405
[SPEAKER_01]: The man would serve him, rising up before... before him, retiring after him, doing whatever he wants, agreeable in his conduct.

368
00:30:23,120 --> 00:30:30,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Indearing in his speech, and that a householder would consider him a friend, a best friend, and he would place trust in him.

369
00:30:31,169 --> 00:30:41,220
[SPEAKER_01]: But when that... when the man becomes aware that the householder was... has placed trust in him, then finding... finding him alone, he would take his life with a sharp knife.

370
00:30:43,850 --> 00:30:44,590
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you think?

371
00:30:44,870 --> 00:30:51,612
[SPEAKER_01]: When that man had approached that householder and said to him, I would serve you, sir, wasn't he a murderer even then?

372
00:30:52,272 --> 00:30:55,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Though the other did not recognize him as my murderer.

373
00:30:56,153 --> 00:31:07,216
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the man was serving him, rising up before him, retiring after him, doing whatever he wants, agreeable in his conduct, endearing in his speech, wasn't he a murderer then too?

374
00:31:07,896 --> 00:31:10,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Though the other did not recognize him as my murderer.

375
00:31:11,559 --> 00:31:20,622
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the man came upon him while he was alone and took his life with a sharp knife, wasn't he a murderer then, too, though the other did not recognize him as my murderer?

376
00:31:22,283 --> 00:31:22,983
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, yes, friend.

377
00:31:23,163 --> 00:31:40,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Likewise, the ordinary person who is not, does not see the noble ones, who is not skilled in their discipline and dhamma, regards form as self, or self as possessing form, or form as in-self, or self as in-form.

378
00:31:41,363 --> 00:31:50,689
[SPEAKER_01]: He does not understand as it really is, impermanent aggregates as impermanent aggregates, or painful aggregates as painful aggregates.

379
00:31:50,709 --> 00:31:51,989
[SPEAKER_04]: So, he places his trust in them.

380
00:31:52,290 --> 00:31:55,051
[SPEAKER_04]: He turns a blind eye to the nature of them.

381
00:31:55,091 --> 00:31:58,774
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't understand murderous aggregates as murderous aggregates.

382
00:31:58,834 --> 00:31:59,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

383
00:32:00,555 --> 00:32:04,977
[SPEAKER_01]: And he becomes engaged with them, clings to them, and takes a stand upon them as myself.

384
00:32:05,698 --> 00:32:07,439
[SPEAKER_04]: And why... why is the simile...

385
00:32:08,927 --> 00:32:09,227
[SPEAKER_04]: It's fitting.

386
00:32:09,367 --> 00:32:14,070
[SPEAKER_04]: Why do you say, let me give you a simple example, then further hammer in what he said.

387
00:32:15,611 --> 00:32:21,555
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is the, why is the simile of a somebody becoming your friend and then killing you?

388
00:32:22,715 --> 00:32:27,838
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is that more fitting than simply saying, yeah, you know that which is yours will kill you type of thing?

389
00:32:29,159 --> 00:32:32,001
[SPEAKER_00]: It pertains to why you

390
00:32:36,100 --> 00:32:41,003
[SPEAKER_00]: You are, let's say, deceived by the... or you don't see the nature of the aggregates.

391
00:32:41,064 --> 00:32:46,387
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not because they're just sort of inherently, kind of ineffably deceptive or something.

392
00:32:46,407 --> 00:32:51,251
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because they're pleasing, held dear, like agreeable.

393
00:32:52,211 --> 00:32:53,752
[SPEAKER_04]: Lull you into safety.

394
00:32:54,913 --> 00:32:55,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Like this body.

395
00:32:55,754 --> 00:32:59,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Just, I mean, just for the body, for example, is the most obvious one.

396
00:33:00,297 --> 00:33:11,540
[SPEAKER_04]: Like when... that's the... when he talks about, like, the intoxication of youth and health and everything, it gives... And where does that intoxication... where does that take place, I suppose?

397
00:33:13,021 --> 00:33:18,622
[SPEAKER_04]: Is that on the level of views, ideas?

398
00:33:18,742 --> 00:33:18,862
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

399
00:33:18,882 --> 00:33:21,163
[SPEAKER_03]: Or it's on the internal level, on the internal relationship?

400
00:33:22,307 --> 00:33:22,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the latter.

401
00:33:23,088 --> 00:33:23,449
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

402
00:33:24,089 --> 00:33:25,231
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's kind of the point.

403
00:33:25,251 --> 00:33:34,723
[SPEAKER_04]: See, as we were saying, the whole... the nature of any self-view is assuming that independent separation being external to all of this.

404
00:33:35,464 --> 00:33:37,086
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if somebody just comes on that level,

405
00:33:37,827 --> 00:33:43,934
[SPEAKER_04]: And tells you, well, all of that is for your harm, as many people would have read this sutta, but not approved to the sense of self.

406
00:33:44,595 --> 00:33:48,098
[SPEAKER_04]: Many people would have understood the simile, to some extent, but not approved to the sense of self.

407
00:33:48,158 --> 00:33:48,539
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is that?

408
00:33:48,559 --> 00:33:55,847
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, because he... and that's the point of the simile, that can be easily overlooked, is... it's not just a question of, oh, he's a murderer, by the way.

409
00:33:56,267 --> 00:33:58,450
[SPEAKER_04]: The question is, he kills you from

410
00:33:59,230 --> 00:34:04,915
[SPEAKER_04]: By entering you in, like, from internally, basically, he approaches you through that internal part that you don't see.

411
00:34:05,516 --> 00:34:12,221
[SPEAKER_04]: Because all you see is, oh, I'm over there, this is mine, I'm projected, I'm projected, but he sort of sneaks past all of that.

412
00:34:12,982 --> 00:34:23,631
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's what somebody, which, you know, everybody who starts, would have these external notions, externally projected, assumes, sends himself independent of the five aggregates.

413
00:34:24,410 --> 00:34:35,339
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if they were to uproot that, they need to first see how these aggregates are first that friend, and then, within that friendship, discern their murderous intent.

414
00:34:35,800 --> 00:34:44,727
[SPEAKER_04]: They can't just externally hear about the murderous intent, because they'll take that another thing with their sense of self externally, and they won't feel affected, they won't feel threatened.

415
00:34:44,747 --> 00:34:50,492
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, I won't feel threatened if I see an external murderer over there, outside of my home, outside of my kingdom.

416
00:34:50,512 --> 00:34:51,273
[SPEAKER_04]: But if suddenly,

417
00:34:52,074 --> 00:35:00,323
[SPEAKER_04]: I recognize that somebody who's been my closest friend, confidant for last 50 years, he's the murderer's intent.

418
00:35:00,643 --> 00:35:05,148
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, now I cannot remain indifferent to that because of my trust I placed in him.

419
00:35:06,149 --> 00:35:09,313
[SPEAKER_04]: Not because he suddenly became a murderer and he wasn't before.

420
00:35:11,153 --> 00:35:14,256
[SPEAKER_04]: And that trust is that always that internal oversight.

421
00:35:14,977 --> 00:35:18,420
[SPEAKER_04]: Always happens internally, when you assume your sense of self externally.

422
00:35:18,581 --> 00:35:28,931
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the problem, like these things are... that they're so close, like the simile doesn't even... it cannot even convey just how close this friend is.

423
00:35:29,231 --> 00:35:31,754
[SPEAKER_04]: You can keep telling to yourself as a mantra that this is all

424
00:35:32,234 --> 00:35:33,755
[SPEAKER_04]: You know, harmful and impermanent.

425
00:35:33,795 --> 00:35:34,756
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly what you said.

426
00:35:34,776 --> 00:35:40,480
[SPEAKER_04]: Like a person would not even know where to look for the aggregates, because when they contemplate the aggregates, that's not what the aggregates are.

427
00:35:40,500 --> 00:35:43,602
[SPEAKER_04]: That's already the result of them having taken up the aggregates.

428
00:35:44,302 --> 00:35:46,464
[SPEAKER_04]: So, and that's same as we said so many times before.

429
00:35:46,484 --> 00:35:47,865
[SPEAKER_04]: You want to contemplate anicca,

430
00:35:49,018 --> 00:35:53,380
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't just, oh, yeah, see, that's impermanent, that's in this carpet, this is impermanent, that's impermanent.

431
00:35:53,520 --> 00:35:54,141
[SPEAKER_04]: It's irrelevant.

432
00:35:54,381 --> 00:35:55,561
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, he's a murderer.

433
00:35:56,122 --> 00:35:57,262
[SPEAKER_04]: None of that makes no difference.

434
00:35:57,542 --> 00:36:10,849
[SPEAKER_04]: No, let me find first things that are my friend, that I hold dearly, that I'm pretty much attached to, that I would not be able to remain indifferent if they change, and let me contemplate them as impermanent, as a threat, as a trap.

435
00:36:11,490 --> 00:36:13,311
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what are those things for people?

436
00:36:14,591 --> 00:36:17,873
[SPEAKER_04]: What would be the most obvious things?

437
00:36:18,650 --> 00:36:19,491
[SPEAKER_04]: Family, exactly.

438
00:36:20,672 --> 00:36:24,935
[SPEAKER_04]: Children, spouses, relatives.

439
00:36:25,015 --> 00:36:27,356
[SPEAKER_04]: What's another thing, in any way?

440
00:36:27,376 --> 00:36:27,557
[SPEAKER_04]: The body.

441
00:36:28,417 --> 00:36:29,598
[SPEAKER_04]: The body, your own body.

442
00:36:30,098 --> 00:36:30,359
[SPEAKER_04]: Health.

443
00:36:30,579 --> 00:36:31,439
[SPEAKER_04]: Health, exactly.

444
00:36:31,460 --> 00:36:34,101
[SPEAKER_04]: Intoxication with health, youth, strength.

445
00:36:34,542 --> 00:36:37,484
[SPEAKER_04]: What other thing, on a level of the world?

446
00:36:37,504 --> 00:36:38,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Reputation.

447
00:36:38,665 --> 00:36:41,027
[SPEAKER_03]: Reputation, yeah, that's the image we have.

448
00:36:41,047 --> 00:36:43,669
[SPEAKER_04]: But wealth?

449
00:36:44,549 --> 00:36:46,651
[SPEAKER_04]: Savings, safety of your home.

450
00:36:47,830 --> 00:36:55,857
[SPEAKER_04]: Safety of your situation, like physical kind of safety, comfort, all of those things, you realize, are taken for granted.

451
00:36:56,958 --> 00:36:59,621
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, well, it's kind of...

452
00:36:59,661 --> 00:37:01,242
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't think it would be very hard for people.

453
00:37:01,282 --> 00:37:06,527
[SPEAKER_04]: If you just even have an inkling against these things, it would be already too frightening.

454
00:37:07,068 --> 00:37:09,390
[SPEAKER_04]: Because most of them, people's concern

455
00:37:10,311 --> 00:37:17,957
[SPEAKER_04]: are directed towards, then, keeping your mind away from thinking these bad thoughts in regard to things that are dear to you.

456
00:37:18,137 --> 00:37:22,781
[SPEAKER_04]: But, how can those thoughts be bad, if they're factual?

457
00:37:23,821 --> 00:37:26,824
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, they can be bad, but that means the facts are bad.

458
00:37:28,732 --> 00:37:31,413
[SPEAKER_04]: The facts are subjected to sickness, aging and death.

459
00:37:31,833 --> 00:37:33,934
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yeah, that is bad.

460
00:37:34,654 --> 00:37:36,835
[SPEAKER_04]: But why is that bad?

461
00:37:37,255 --> 00:37:43,197
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it a problem that this person, who pretended to be my friend, was a murderer?

462
00:37:43,298 --> 00:37:44,198
[SPEAKER_04]: Is that my problem?

463
00:37:44,238 --> 00:37:45,338
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is that my problem?

464
00:37:46,579 --> 00:37:49,760
[SPEAKER_04]: When does it become my problem that he wants to kill me?

465
00:37:52,303 --> 00:37:54,005
[SPEAKER_02]: When you delight in that friendship.

466
00:37:54,065 --> 00:37:55,166
[SPEAKER_04]: When you like him in the house.

467
00:37:56,527 --> 00:37:58,228
[SPEAKER_04]: When you take him as a friend.

468
00:37:58,689 --> 00:38:00,190
[SPEAKER_04]: When you don't discern his motives.

469
00:38:00,750 --> 00:38:05,455
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you don't do any of that, his murderous intent is not your problem.

470
00:38:05,595 --> 00:38:09,818
[SPEAKER_04]: It cannot murder you, because he hasn't gained your confidence, your trust.

471
00:38:11,180 --> 00:38:14,402
[SPEAKER_04]: But again, you can't...

472
00:38:16,110 --> 00:38:21,312
[SPEAKER_03]: The situation you find yourself in, you can't not let him in, because he's in already.

473
00:38:21,412 --> 00:38:29,136
[SPEAKER_04]: Of course, we're talking, yeah, we're talking, like, on the level of theory, so people can wrap their head around, but, yeah, you don't start in a neutral position.

474
00:38:29,696 --> 00:38:38,080
[SPEAKER_04]: You already start with, well, five closest friends, and they're all here competing who's going to kill you first, and you don't even see it.

475
00:38:39,120 --> 00:38:42,462
[SPEAKER_04]: You still plan further, engage further,

476
00:38:43,481 --> 00:38:51,965
[SPEAKER_04]: On account of their friendship, and you are actually becoming even more dependent on their presence, yet their entire purpose of their presence is to take you out.

477
00:38:54,127 --> 00:38:54,407
[SPEAKER_03]: So...

478
00:38:54,747 --> 00:38:59,870
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking, just in relation to the

479
00:39:01,932 --> 00:39:06,353
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessarily so easy to see the things that are so close.

480
00:39:06,994 --> 00:39:08,034
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, family.

481
00:39:08,474 --> 00:39:13,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can think about, I don't know, your mother or something, that is dear to you.

482
00:39:13,916 --> 00:39:20,578
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, if she dies, when people's family, a close family, die, you might feel grief.

483
00:39:21,878 --> 00:39:41,133
[SPEAKER_00]: But what it doesn't usually occur to you is that even the grief on account of your mother dying and the thought of, like, that she's still, even she's dead, she's my mother, my mother is dead, that that is in a way even more dear.

484
00:39:42,340 --> 00:39:45,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the root of the dearness.

485
00:39:45,422 --> 00:39:46,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

486
00:39:46,443 --> 00:39:49,025
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why you can grieve even after the person's gone.

487
00:39:49,405 --> 00:39:59,792
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not because, you know, whether she's alive or she's dead, she is still dear as my mother, as like something that makes me who I am.

488
00:40:00,232 --> 00:40:01,493
[SPEAKER_04]: So how do you then approve that?

489
00:40:02,554 --> 00:40:05,095
[SPEAKER_00]: By contemplating the end of that.

490
00:40:05,215 --> 00:40:07,437
[SPEAKER_04]: And what is the end of that?

491
00:40:07,457 --> 00:40:08,077
[SPEAKER_04]: Stop thinking it?

492
00:40:08,097 --> 00:40:08,938
[SPEAKER_04]: Would that be an end of it?

493
00:40:09,648 --> 00:40:10,328
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

494
00:40:10,448 --> 00:40:18,872
[SPEAKER_00]: It's as in, like, that you could lose even that, like, that memory, for example, or that... because even that can change.

495
00:40:19,532 --> 00:40:21,173
[SPEAKER_04]: How would you lose that memory?

496
00:40:21,213 --> 00:40:21,653
[SPEAKER_04]: Alzheimer's?

497
00:40:21,933 --> 00:40:24,374
[SPEAKER_04]: But why would then... you won't suffer from candidate if you don't remember it?

498
00:40:25,194 --> 00:40:26,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Alzheimer's is pretty scary.

499
00:40:27,175 --> 00:40:28,676
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it's scary when you don't have it.

500
00:40:29,905 --> 00:40:32,527
[SPEAKER_04]: When you have it, you've lost perspective on it.

501
00:40:32,867 --> 00:40:33,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but that isn't...

502
00:40:33,568 --> 00:40:39,673
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you could say the same for, like, oh, well, I... you know, for death, you know, like death... Yeah, that's exactly what people say.

503
00:40:39,993 --> 00:40:40,674
[SPEAKER_04]: That's exactly...

504
00:40:40,694 --> 00:40:41,154
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why?

505
00:40:41,535 --> 00:40:45,738
[SPEAKER_04]: How... how... like, because it's obviously not enough if they just... because that's exactly what they will fall into.

506
00:40:45,758 --> 00:40:47,660
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, I will die when I die.

507
00:40:48,160 --> 00:40:48,540
[SPEAKER_04]: That's fine.

508
00:40:49,761 --> 00:40:51,583
[SPEAKER_04]: Why... why scare myself with it now?

509
00:40:52,818 --> 00:40:54,519
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's a sort of stoic position.

510
00:40:54,579 --> 00:40:56,340
[SPEAKER_03]: Life and death cannot be there together.

511
00:40:56,620 --> 00:40:57,961
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when I'm dead, I'm dead.

512
00:40:58,061 --> 00:40:59,622
[SPEAKER_03]: So, why worry about it when I'm alive?

513
00:41:00,082 --> 00:41:02,023
[SPEAKER_04]: And so, you sort of rationalize the whole problem.

514
00:41:02,043 --> 00:41:04,904
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why worry about it?

515
00:41:04,964 --> 00:41:06,025
[SPEAKER_04]: Why should you worry about it?

516
00:41:06,105 --> 00:41:08,006
[SPEAKER_04]: Why should you worry about exactly even that?

517
00:41:08,586 --> 00:41:10,607
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, my mother that has passed away is still dear to me.

518
00:41:10,747 --> 00:41:15,290
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, why is that then still a problem, that thought?

519
00:41:15,430 --> 00:41:16,350
[SPEAKER_03]: Why should I worry about it?

520
00:41:16,370 --> 00:41:17,431
[SPEAKER_03]: Why should I think about death?

521
00:41:19,450 --> 00:41:22,772
[SPEAKER_04]: If it's not here yet, then when it's here, well, then I can't do anything about it.

522
00:41:24,452 --> 00:41:25,273
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why?

523
00:41:25,373 --> 00:41:26,233
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is it beneficial?

524
00:41:27,794 --> 00:41:28,915
[SPEAKER_04]: Like we saw, because we are Buddhists.

525
00:41:28,935 --> 00:41:29,955
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, put that aside.

526
00:41:30,836 --> 00:41:32,296
[SPEAKER_04]: But why is it beneficial?

527
00:41:32,416 --> 00:41:35,058
[SPEAKER_04]: Factually, it is beneficial to think about it.

528
00:41:35,498 --> 00:41:38,059
[SPEAKER_04]: And what would then the benefit be?

529
00:41:38,339 --> 00:41:39,180
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is it beneficial?

530
00:41:39,200 --> 00:41:40,180
[SPEAKER_04]: Why you should think about it?

531
00:41:42,121 --> 00:41:43,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it results in, well...

532
00:41:44,760 --> 00:41:48,343
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is it not beneficial to say, well, I'll die when I die.

533
00:41:48,764 --> 00:41:50,005
[SPEAKER_04]: In the meantime, I won't think about it.

534
00:41:50,025 --> 00:41:51,266
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you're still suffering.

535
00:41:51,826 --> 00:41:52,907
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that's not what death is.

536
00:41:53,768 --> 00:41:57,311
[SPEAKER_04]: What you think death is, what you think losing the loved one is, isn't.

537
00:41:58,352 --> 00:42:02,436
[SPEAKER_04]: It's based on you already being intoxicated, already having the ownership.

538
00:42:02,456 --> 00:42:03,597
[SPEAKER_04]: So, when you think about death,

539
00:42:04,635 --> 00:42:10,557
[SPEAKER_04]: You might feel initially that... but even that takes effort to sustain, that initial anxiety in the face of it.

540
00:42:11,277 --> 00:42:15,138
[SPEAKER_04]: And if you're not careful, you will just fall onto another view, external view.

541
00:42:15,178 --> 00:42:16,339
[SPEAKER_04]: Our death will come to this.

542
00:42:17,599 --> 00:42:21,740
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't feel affected, factually, because you already projected yourself externally.

543
00:42:22,140 --> 00:42:25,922
[SPEAKER_04]: You're not seeing it on that internal, intimate level of my friendship.

544
00:42:26,622 --> 00:42:26,822
[SPEAKER_04]: So,

545
00:42:27,970 --> 00:42:33,432
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly what people would, because it's unavoidable for them to think or experience losing the loved ones.

546
00:42:33,472 --> 00:42:36,153
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet, most of them still don't turn to dhamma.

547
00:42:36,873 --> 00:42:37,353
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is that?

548
00:42:37,413 --> 00:42:42,135
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, because the grief... you cope with it by assuming another external point of view to it.

549
00:42:43,535 --> 00:42:52,919
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you were honest, even then, you recognize, well... See, the problem of me not thinking about death is because, first and foremost,

550
00:42:53,607 --> 00:42:54,748
[SPEAKER_04]: Do I even know what death is?

551
00:42:55,288 --> 00:42:57,009
[SPEAKER_04]: And to what extent I understand it?

552
00:42:57,250 --> 00:42:59,711
[SPEAKER_04]: To what extent I've just projected another idea onto it?

553
00:43:00,492 --> 00:43:13,221
[SPEAKER_04]: And secondly, if I think I will be unmoved by death, whenever it comes, or it won't be a bigger problem, surely then, I shouldn't be moved by anything else that's inferior to that death.

554
00:43:13,321 --> 00:43:15,803
[SPEAKER_04]: All these little... well, he said this, she said that.

555
00:43:16,223 --> 00:43:17,984
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet, I'm very moved by those things.

556
00:43:18,124 --> 00:43:21,627
[SPEAKER_04]: I do get annoyed, I get lustful, I get full of desire, I get full of anger.

557
00:43:22,508 --> 00:43:23,048
[SPEAKER_04]: But somehow,

558
00:43:23,988 --> 00:43:31,332
[SPEAKER_04]: The utmost, you know, bad experience of dying, disintegrating, pain, falling apart, but I will be unmoved in regard to that.

559
00:43:31,432 --> 00:43:33,133
[SPEAKER_04]: Is that a realistic expectation?

560
00:43:33,613 --> 00:43:46,679
[SPEAKER_04]: If I'm moved on account of sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, surely I will be moved when those organs that provide me with sight, sound, smell, taste, touches are actually breaking apart and I'm witnessing that.

561
00:43:46,699 --> 00:43:51,902
[SPEAKER_04]: So it's like, okay, so first and foremost, I probably have a very inaccurate idea of what death is.

562
00:43:53,710 --> 00:43:56,092
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, secondly, exactly, I still suffer here and now.

563
00:43:57,052 --> 00:43:58,733
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I can keep turning a blind eye to it.

564
00:43:59,533 --> 00:44:04,456
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, your relative passes, you grieve, and then you stop grieving.

565
00:44:04,516 --> 00:44:05,096
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is that?

566
00:44:06,237 --> 00:44:08,498
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you... Why did you suffer in the first place?

567
00:44:10,959 --> 00:44:14,341
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you don't suffer when somebody else passes, that's irrelevant to you.

568
00:44:15,321 --> 00:44:17,042
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why did you suffer in the first place?

569
00:44:18,080 --> 00:44:19,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it was your relative.

570
00:44:19,500 --> 00:44:19,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

571
00:44:20,020 --> 00:44:25,261
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yeah, it was basically your thing was subjected to change that you didn't want.

572
00:44:26,282 --> 00:44:27,522
[SPEAKER_04]: And why don't you suffer now?

573
00:44:27,822 --> 00:44:30,582
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it because you removed the ownership of these things?

574
00:44:30,742 --> 00:44:33,743
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you obviously didn't, because you still think of it as my relative, my mother.

575
00:44:34,123 --> 00:44:35,323
[SPEAKER_04]: But why don't you suffer now?

576
00:44:36,364 --> 00:44:37,324
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it because you understood it?

577
00:44:37,844 --> 00:44:43,445
[SPEAKER_04]: Or because you just buried it again with the distraction of the sense objects and sense organs and pleasure you get through it?

578
00:44:45,187 --> 00:44:51,693
[SPEAKER_04]: So, and that's exactly why people then, who have lost their loved ones, cope with it, dealt with it, continue with their life.

579
00:44:52,293 --> 00:45:06,807
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, if they were to then go, say, on a retreat, or a more secluded environment, withdrawn from sense objects and distractions and pleasures, all those previously coped things will come back completely unresolved.

580
00:45:07,307 --> 00:45:09,990
[SPEAKER_04]: And not just passing away, anything unpleasant you experienced.

581
00:45:10,645 --> 00:45:18,967
[SPEAKER_04]: Because unenlightened mind, as the Buddha said so many times, has no other means of dealing with pain other than engaging with sensuality and burying it under the pain.

582
00:45:19,007 --> 00:45:21,408
[SPEAKER_04]: You bury it under pleasure, pleasure, perpetual.

583
00:45:21,468 --> 00:45:29,010
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, the more you do it, the more you're dependent on that pleasure that's already quite burdening and hard to access, or hard to sustain, and so on.

584
00:45:29,810 --> 00:45:31,111
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's kind of the point.

585
00:45:31,131 --> 00:45:34,072
[SPEAKER_04]: You recognize that, well, wait, why did I suffer in the first place?

586
00:45:34,132 --> 00:45:35,592
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure, I don't suffer anymore.

587
00:45:35,872 --> 00:45:36,572
[SPEAKER_04]: I dealt with it.

588
00:45:37,273 --> 00:45:37,853
[SPEAKER_04]: She's gone.

589
00:45:38,013 --> 00:45:38,513
[SPEAKER_04]: It's fine.

590
00:45:39,091 --> 00:45:40,792
[SPEAKER_04]: But why did I suffer in the first place?

591
00:45:41,212 --> 00:45:45,055
[SPEAKER_04]: If I suffered on account of that in the first place, can I suffer on account of other things?

592
00:45:45,475 --> 00:45:45,915
[SPEAKER_04]: And then what?

593
00:45:46,235 --> 00:45:47,696
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, then I'll have to deal with it.

594
00:45:48,177 --> 00:45:51,739
[SPEAKER_04]: So then I'll be burdened with having to cope, manage.

595
00:45:52,379 --> 00:45:55,621
[SPEAKER_04]: But is it possible to not suffer in the first place?

596
00:45:56,302 --> 00:45:58,883
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it possible to not even be touched by that suffering?

597
00:45:58,923 --> 00:46:00,444
[SPEAKER_04]: So I have nothing to deal with.

598
00:46:00,464 --> 00:46:01,405
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the true freedom.

599
00:46:01,505 --> 00:46:03,346
[SPEAKER_04]: There is no debt I have to pay back then.

600
00:46:04,306 --> 00:46:05,027
[SPEAKER_04]: Or work off.

601
00:46:06,183 --> 00:46:13,250
[SPEAKER_04]: And, yeah, if you're then honest, you recognize, okay, so you didn't really suffer because this form changed, mother passed away or something.

602
00:46:13,270 --> 00:46:16,814
[SPEAKER_04]: You suffered because you were taking that which cannot be yours is yours.

603
00:46:17,835 --> 00:46:21,038
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so am I still doing that in regard to other things?

604
00:46:21,439 --> 00:46:22,400
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, hell yeah!

605
00:46:23,346 --> 00:46:29,109
[SPEAKER_04]: Which means, when those things change, I will then suffer, if not the same, maybe probably even more.

606
00:46:29,609 --> 00:46:40,395
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, see, if an external form was dear to you, imagine how much more your own form, your own organs, that you used to get the pleasure and distraction and so on, to experience all these other secondary things.

607
00:46:40,415 --> 00:46:42,116
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's even more dear to you.

608
00:46:42,136 --> 00:46:44,037
[SPEAKER_04]: So, imagine how much more you will suffer then.

609
00:46:45,199 --> 00:46:58,684
[SPEAKER_04]: But again, not because those things that are subject to change would change, but because those things that are subject to change, well, you take up as yours by ignoring that they're subject to change, by turning a blind eye to engaging through sensuality and distraction.

610
00:46:58,964 --> 00:46:59,304
[SPEAKER_04]: That's it.

611
00:47:01,445 --> 00:47:03,806
[SPEAKER_02]: So, death is presently enduring.

612
00:47:03,926 --> 00:47:05,907
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not something in the future.

613
00:47:06,347 --> 00:47:08,008
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but in order for that,

614
00:47:09,249 --> 00:47:21,821
[SPEAKER_04]: To be understood, really, that the weight of that person first needs to... Well, again, through some honesty, you recognize, okay, so the ownership, taking up things is there, that's the root of suffering, I should really aim to not... to be free from suffering.

615
00:47:21,841 --> 00:47:25,665
[SPEAKER_04]: Because if they don't do that, then they just adopt another external view in regard to death.

616
00:47:25,945 --> 00:47:27,086
[SPEAKER_04]: And death won't apply to them.

617
00:47:27,126 --> 00:47:31,450
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why I mentioned what I did in regard to that simile Venerable Sariputra gave.

618
00:47:31,470 --> 00:47:33,172
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, it's not, oh, yeah, it's a murder.

619
00:47:33,212 --> 00:47:34,052
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, I'll be murdered.

620
00:47:34,092 --> 00:47:34,573
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm fine.

621
00:47:35,250 --> 00:47:35,590
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no, no.

622
00:47:35,610 --> 00:47:44,796
[SPEAKER_04]: You've got to recognize that that that person who is saying, ah, you, yourself, those aggregates, those perceptions, that's already there murdering you.

623
00:47:45,116 --> 00:47:48,838
[SPEAKER_04]: So, your thought of you'll be fine is another trap that will murder you.

624
00:47:50,219 --> 00:47:56,763
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not... you have no external standpoint, which is always assumed by saying, oh, well, when death comes, that's it.

625
00:47:57,383 --> 00:48:04,067
[SPEAKER_04]: Ah, so, basically, you kind of imply, through your own assumption of existence, of the external standpoint, that you won't be affected by it.

626
00:48:05,570 --> 00:48:06,130
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's fine.

627
00:48:06,211 --> 00:48:09,353
[SPEAKER_04]: You cannot get rid of that notion, but then you start including that notion.

628
00:48:09,433 --> 00:48:16,898
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so when I say death will come, whenever it comes, I'll deal with it then, and I feel safe on account of that thought here and now.

629
00:48:17,399 --> 00:48:26,145
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, but there is another five aggregates here and now, that I'm taking as safe, on account of admitting that they are root of complete non-safety.

630
00:48:27,486 --> 00:48:35,072
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you're honest and just push the same principle as far as it goes, you will realize, okay, yeah, all of this that I know is that

631
00:48:35,649 --> 00:48:37,390
[SPEAKER_04]: Murderer that pretends to be my friend.

632
00:48:37,870 --> 00:48:41,772
[SPEAKER_04]: Form, feelings, perceptions, intentions, awareness, consciousness.

633
00:48:41,972 --> 00:48:42,292
[SPEAKER_04]: That's it.

634
00:48:43,073 --> 00:48:43,393
[SPEAKER_04]: All of it.

635
00:48:44,373 --> 00:48:45,434
[SPEAKER_04]: All of it is the friend.

636
00:48:46,434 --> 00:49:04,443
[SPEAKER_04]: All of it is the murderer that pretends to be my friend and lulls me into sense of safety by saying, oh, no, death will come to everyone, you know, no problem, by providing me with these external self, external notions of self, views, wrong views in regard to the aggregates and everything else, so that I don't see him for what it is.

637
00:49:06,392 --> 00:49:08,794
[SPEAKER_04]: Making you believe, basically, threat is over there.

638
00:49:09,234 --> 00:49:10,796
[SPEAKER_04]: You avoid it by moving over there.

639
00:49:11,056 --> 00:49:16,180
[SPEAKER_04]: Either way, keeping you external to the truth of, he's the one there, plotting for it.

640
00:49:17,742 --> 00:49:18,983
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the danger of views.

641
00:49:19,783 --> 00:49:22,165
[SPEAKER_04]: It always requires the external, assumption of external.

642
00:49:22,586 --> 00:49:23,847
[SPEAKER_04]: Even the right view, for that matter.

643
00:49:24,868 --> 00:49:27,190
[SPEAKER_04]: But, the right view does point back to itself.

644
00:49:27,390 --> 00:49:32,694
[SPEAKER_04]: So, eventually, you can, you can, you can uproot any notion of external, through using that right view.

645
00:49:34,592 --> 00:49:38,676
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's how arahant, as I said before, goes beyond the views altogether.

646
00:49:39,297 --> 00:49:45,523
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, they're not the slightest trace of any... like even possibility of assuming external to the aggregates.

647
00:49:47,685 --> 00:49:49,947
[SPEAKER_04]: Assuming independence in regard to the aggregates.

648
00:49:55,108 --> 00:50:02,313
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, assuming, like, what we were saying, like, health, wealth, family, all of these assumptions... Yeah.

649
00:50:02,453 --> 00:50:03,293
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you start with that.

650
00:50:03,413 --> 00:50:10,698
[SPEAKER_04]: Say a person starts with that, and then, they inevitably gonna have some coping mechanism on the mental level.

651
00:50:13,099 --> 00:50:15,321
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you include that as well.

652
00:50:15,601 --> 00:50:16,902
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, this coping mechanism.

653
00:50:18,102 --> 00:50:19,243
[SPEAKER_04]: Can that be independent?

654
00:50:19,628 --> 00:50:22,709
[SPEAKER_04]: of this feeling, of these things that are subjected to change.

655
00:50:23,209 --> 00:50:28,270
[SPEAKER_04]: So, my coping sense of safety, well, that's another trap that's actually unsafe.

656
00:50:29,750 --> 00:50:31,490
[SPEAKER_04]: So, no place to hide.

657
00:50:33,370 --> 00:50:39,151
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like when the Buddha said, anybody who believes that they have health is a fool.

658
00:50:39,772 --> 00:50:40,212
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

659
00:50:41,232 --> 00:50:47,653
[SPEAKER_04]: You're just... Or anybody who believes that not having health is not a problem is also a fool.

660
00:50:48,387 --> 00:50:49,328
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, fine, I'll deal with it.

661
00:50:50,768 --> 00:50:51,829
[SPEAKER_04]: No, because they're intoxicated.

662
00:50:52,409 --> 00:50:56,051
[SPEAKER_04]: That's a drunk person, doesn't care about the consequences of his actions, because he's drunk.

663
00:50:56,852 --> 00:50:59,873
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, when he sobers up, he realizes, oh my God, what have I done?

664
00:51:01,254 --> 00:51:04,456
[SPEAKER_04]: But there is no way of talking that into him, while he's drunk.

665
00:51:05,656 --> 00:51:06,277
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't matter.

666
00:51:07,737 --> 00:51:08,578
[SPEAKER_04]: Loss of perspective.

667
00:51:08,878 --> 00:51:10,679
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you realize, that's the peril.

668
00:51:10,779 --> 00:51:13,661
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm already in a state of lack of perspective.

669
00:51:14,521 --> 00:51:15,902
[SPEAKER_02]: Of death, of that being death?

670
00:51:15,922 --> 00:51:17,623
[SPEAKER_04]: Health, death, loss.

671
00:51:18,600 --> 00:51:23,904
[SPEAKER_04]: And you will know that if you simply ask yourself, well, can I suffer on account of anything?

672
00:51:27,286 --> 00:51:31,068
[SPEAKER_04]: Can suffering arise on account of anything that is able to change?

673
00:51:32,069 --> 00:51:35,431
[SPEAKER_04]: Or can I not suffer on account of anything, any change?

674
00:51:37,132 --> 00:51:45,098
[SPEAKER_04]: Can I remain imperturbable, composed, regardless of how much things change, whether they're dear to me or not?

675
00:51:45,658 --> 00:51:48,240
[SPEAKER_04]: If I lose everything this second, would I be affected or not?

